    ('22481', 0, $$This book was made possible through the$$),
    ('22481', 1, $$Author: Kenneth Youngstein$$),
    ('22481', 2, $$If you walk down the red clay road and$$),
    ('22481', 3, $$Here lives Thembi, her brother, Bongani, and$$),
    ('22481', 4, $$Gogo is in the kitchen, cooking dinner. From$$),
    ('22481', 5, $$Gogo takes off her apron and goes$$),
    ('22481', 6, $$Gogo finds Thembi sitting$$),
    ('22481', 7, $$Gogo asks Thembi, "Why are you sitting here, all alone? " "Oh," replies Thembi. "The other children don't want to play with me. "$$),
    ('22481', 8, $$At the clinic, they meet Mr. Ntuli. He asks Thembi to$$),
    ('22481', 9, $$"What about playing with your friend Sizani? " asks Gogo. "Sizani is mad at me," replies Thembi. "She said that last week she waved to me and I ignored her. Gogo, I really didn't see her. "$$),
    ('22481', 10, $$"What about playing with your brother? " asks Gogo.$$),
    ('22481', 11, $$"But aren't you lonely? " asks Gogo. "Oh no," replies Thembi. "I love sitting here, listening to the tree sing its beautiful songs. Listen, can't you hear it? "$$),
    ('22481', 12, $$Gogo listens and then looks up into the tree and sees a beautiful kingfisher, a cuckoo, and a robin, all singing their songs.$$),
    ('22481', 13, $$Gogo remembered Thembi's teacher telling her that$$),
    ('22481', 14, $$At the clinic, they meet Mr. Ntuli. He asks Thembi to$$),
    ('22481', 15, $$Thembi looks and looks and says, "I can see$$),
    ('22481', 16, $$Mr. Ntuli puts a metal frame on Thembi's nose.$$),
    ('22481', 17, $$"I can see," shouts Thembi. "I can see the letter E and F and P and even the little letters, P, Z, and D. " "That's perfect," says Mr. Ntuli. "All you need are spectacles, and you will see just fine. "$$),
    ('22481', 18, $$The nurse takes Thembi and Gogo to another room$$),
    ('22481', 19, $$After one week, Thembi and Gogo return to Mr. Ntuli's office and Thembi tries her new spectacles.$$),
    ('22481', 20, $$"Thembi," says Mr. Ntuli, "I want you to promise me that you will wear your spectacles every day. " "Oh yes," replies Thembi. "I promise. I promise. I love my spectacles and all the wonderful things I can see. "$$),
    ('22481', 21, $$As Gogo and Thembi walk home, Thembi sees Sizani$$),
    ('22481', 22, $$Just as Gogo and Thembi reach their house, they meet some of Thembi's friends. "Oh Thembi, I love your spectacles," says one girl. "They make you look very smart," comments one of the boys. "Maybe I also need spectacles? " remarks another.$$),
    ('22481', 23, $$All of a sudden, a ball comes rolling towards Thembi.$$),
    ('22481', 24, $$Thembi walks behind the house and stands under the large tree. As she looks up, she can see the kingfisher, the cuckoo and the robin.$$),
    ('22481', 25, $$She puts her arms around the tree and with a big smile, says, "Thank you tree. Thank you for singing such beautiful songs to me. "$$),
    ('22481', 26, $$Author Kenneth Youngstein has spent forty years developing educational programs for healthcare professionals and patients throughout the world.$$),
    ('22510', 0, $$Once upon a time, Pa Nase the spider felt very hungry. He decided to go in search of food. He visited his friend's place where he usually got help.$$),
    ('22510', 1, $$Hungry Pa Nase thought of an idea for getting food. He remembered the store where the village women usually kept food.$$),
    ('22510', 2, $$He sneaked into the store and stole some fish and bananas.$$),
    ('22510', 3, $$On his way out, the little children noticed his unusual movements. They called on one of the women.$$),
    ('22510', 4, $$With loaded pockets, Pa Nase was very afraid and wanted to run.$$),
    ('22510', 5, $$"Let us grind him on the village stone," Yalol, one of the women suggested.$$),
    ('22510', 6, $$Pa Nase laughed out loud and said, "My grandfather lives in this rock. Grinding me on it is like sending me home. Thank you! "$$),
    ('22510', 7, $$"Let us throw him in the fire," said Yangbet.$$),
    ('22510', 8, $$"Let us fill a basket with fruit, put him inside and throw it into the river" Yabana suggested. At this, Pa Nase cried, "You have finally killed me! "$$),
    ('22510', 9, $$Once in the middle of the river, Pa Nase celebrated. He took some fruit and ate as he laughed at the women.$$),
    ('22510', 10, $$The women felt tricked and tried to hook Pa Nase.$$),
    ('22510', 11, $$Pa Nase forgot that there was fishing net down the river. He was caught in the net and got trapped.$$),
    ('22542', 0, $$Three siblings, Udoo, Erdoo and Eryum listened to the music of the rain. They wanted to dance in it. They wanted to touch the rainbow that arrived in the sky. Mother said, "No. "$$),
    ('22542', 1, $$Mother caught him before he could go. She raised her voice so that Eryum and his sisters would hear too.$$),
    ('22542', 2, $$Hoping that they would ask for a story, she said, "The rainbow is a magical river with healing powers. But it is so high up in the sky that you can't reach it. If you catch a cold, the rainbow can't help you. "$$),
    ('22542', 3, $$"Mother, please tell us our rainbow story," said Erdoo. "Yes, please mother, I want to hear it too," Udoo chimed in. "Me three," Eryum begged, lifting up three little fingers.$$),
    ('22542', 4, $$Erdoo ran for the naha, the stirring stick they used as their totem. She handed it over to mother. Eryum got the headgear for mother to wear. It never failed to put her into character.$$),
    ('22542', 5, $$"Once, the rainbow was a magical river. It was hidden inside the green woods of Mbadede. Because it had healing powers, it was guarded.$$),
    ('22542', 6, $$"Because of the river's magic, there was ice cream along the banks! Everyone who came to drink the water also enjoyed the ice cream, especially children.$$),
    ('22542', 7, $$"One day, a naughty old woman called Mbom came from the land beyond. On arrival, she met with a guard. He did not know her and he felt a strange feeling. But he did not listen to his feelings.$$),
    ('22542', 8, $$"She took a drink and was healed. Then she looked around to make sure she wasn't being watched.$$),
    ('22542', 9, $$"Mbom jumped right into the water! She hoped to get everlasting life. As soon as she jumped, the river rose up. It shot high into the sky, never to return to Mbadede. But sometimes the rainbow follows after the rain, to feel like a river again.$$),
    ('22542', 10, $$"So my lovelies tell me, why do you love this story so much? asked mother. "You often ask me to tell it. "$$),
    ('22542', 11, $$Eryum tried to push his luck, "I love it because it reminds me of ice cream! Can I have some now, pleeeaaase? "$$),
    ('22550', 0, $$One day after a heavy rain, Iman looked across the sky and saw colours in it for the first time.$$),
    ('22550', 1, $$How beautiful, she thought. Someone must have worked very hard, painting the sky with a brush like Daddy paints houses.$$),
    ('22550', 2, $$She ran to her sister and said, "Zarah look! Someone has painted the sky for us and made it beautiful. "$$),
    ('22550', 3, $$But Zarah said, "No, silly. That is a rainbow. You are little and I am big. I know why it is in the sky. Let me show you. "$$),
    ('22550', 4, $$Zarah took Iman to the zoo. She said, "Rainbows do not appear because lion cubs roar for the first time or because elephants are born. "$$),
    ('22550', 5, $$She took Iman to a field of flowers and said, "They do not appear because butterflies lose their colours in the clouds. "$$),
    ('22550', 6, $$She showed Iman a book. She said, "They do not even appear because rhinos run across the ground and scare colours up into the sky. "$$),
    ('22550', 7, $$"I know why rainbows really appear. Mummy told me and now I'll tell you. "$$),
    ('22550', 8, $$"Rainbows appear in the sky because of children like you and me. One day, colours looked down from above and liked what they could see. "$$),
    ('22550', 9, $$"They saw children with light skin, dark skin and everything in between. They thought, 'How wonderful it must be to be as beautiful as these. '"$$),
    ('22550', 10, $$"They came together in the sky and spoke about what they could do. They decided, 'If we are to be beautiful we must do it as a group. '"$$),
    ('22550', 11, $$"Now they join together and shine as brightly as they can. Hoping to be as beautiful as the children they see, just like you and me. "$$),
    ('22565', 0, $$In a particular year, the dry season was too long and a drought hit hard. As a result, all the rivers dried up, except the River of Blessings.$$),
    ('22565', 1, $$Camel was the first to speak, "As you know, we are very thirsty and all the rivers have dried up, except the River of Blessings. But it is very far away. What do we do? "$$),
    ('22565', 2, $$Horse suggested, "Some of us can go to the River of Blessings. We can drink and bring water for the others. "$$),
    ('22565', 3, $$Sheep said, "Maa! Maaa! I will go to the River of Blessings and drink water. "$$),
    ('22565', 4, $$All the animals wanted to go. Camel led the way.$$),
    ('22565', 5, $$They continued. Chicken said, "Please, I am tired, I no longer want to go to the River of Blessings. "$$),
    ('22565', 6, $$As the journey continued, Sheep also became tired.$$),
    ('22565', 7, $$Soon, Goat was too tired to continue going to the river. Horse turned and swallowed Goat.$$),
    ('22565', 8, $$When Cow finally could not continue, Camel turned and swallowed her.$$),
    ('22565', 9, $$Being the only animal left, Camel did her best to reach the River of Blessings.$$),
    ('22565', 10, $$On arrival, Camel lay down and vomited Cow.$$),
    ('22565', 11, $$And that is how all the animals managed to drink water from the River of Blessings.$$),
    ('22599', 0, $$Once upon a time, there lived a prince who had no name from birth. He was the only child of King Dunoma, who wanted a successor. He was born after several years of waiting.$$),
    ('22599', 1, $$When the prince was old enough to marry, the king ordered the town crier to make an announcement.$$),
    ('22599', 2, $$The girls devoted the remaining days to preparation. They plaited their hair and trimmed their nails. They decorated their hands and feet with henna, and went to the tailor for new dresses.$$),
    ('22599', 3, $$On the appointed day, all roads led to the king's palace. The prince and his father, accompanied by the palace guards, arrived at the assembly ground first.$$),
    ('22599', 4, $$Everything was set. The town crier announced the commencement of the event. Each girl would step forward and say three names.$$),
    ('22599', 5, $$The first girl stepped forward and said, "Is it Modu? Is it Mommodu? Is it Mattaa? "$$),
    ('22599', 6, $$The second girl stepped forward and said, "Is it Bukar? Is it Umar? Is it Usman? "$$),
    ('22599', 7, $$The third girl stepped forward and said, "Is it Ali? Is it Zubair? Is it Abba? "$$),
    ('22599', 8, $$There was one girl who was encouraged by her mother to participate. When she left, her mother wished her good luck.$$),
    ('22599', 9, $$The girl comforted the old woman and plaited her hair. The old woman asked, "My child, where are you going? "$$),
    ('22599', 10, $$The girl thanked the old woman and left for the venue. When she arrived, she was surprised to see such a large gathering. She felt shy and could not go forward.$$),
    ('22599', 11, $$She stepped forward and began, "Is it Ibrahim? Is it Kassim? Is it Kagu? " The prince said, "You have not got it my sister. "$$),
    ('22602', 0, $$He is not expecting visitors.$$),
    ('22602', 1, $$The man rushes to his neighbour.$$),
    ('22602', 2, $$The neighbour responds, "Do not bother me. The door is shut, and we are all in bed. I cannot give you anything. "$$),
    ('22602', 3, $$But the first man continues to knock on his neighbour's door.$$),
    ('22602', 4, $$And so, the man was able to return to his friends with bread.$$),
    ('22642', 0, $$There once lived a king in the southern part of Okugbe Island. This king had not known pain in his life.$$),
    ('22642', 1, $$One morning he was imagining what pain would be like. His head was bowed, "There are so many poor people in my land. How can I feel their pain? " he asked himself.$$),
    ('22642', 2, $$Day and night, he wished he could experience pain just once.$$),
    ('22642', 3, $$The voice continued, "Build seven large boats that can contain all your wealth and family. Then sail away to the northern part of Okugbe Island. "$$),
    ('22642', 4, $$Soon, the seven boats were built and ready to sail. The king loaded them with his wealth.$$),
    ('22642', 5, $$The day before the king and his family left, he held a feast. Everyone attended. Leopards, snakes, rabbits, snails, birds and even ants came to the feast.$$),
    ('22642', 6, $$The king addressed the people, "My family and I are going away to the other part of Okugbe Island. We want to feel the pain that other people feel. "$$),
    ('22642', 7, $$People whispered amongst themselves. The elders did not believe him.$$),
    ('22642', 8, $$The next day, the king and his family sailed away.$$),
    ('22642', 9, $$On the third day, the sea became rough. The sky became cloudy. A great storm came upon them.$$),
    ('22642', 10, $$The king alone survived. But he was now a kingfisher bird.$$),
    ('22642', 11, $$Kingfisher dived into the sea many times. He was not able to rescue his family or even part of his wealth.$$),
    ('22659', 0, $$Mummy cooked food in a cooking pot. She locked it up in the kitchen so that she could catch up with her customers at the market.$$),
    ('22659', 1, $$Ann and her two brothers came back from Ntuuse$$),
    ('22659', 2, $$Junior cried of hunger but Ann told him that they should all wait for mummy to come back from the market. Junior sneaked into the kitchen and ate the food.$$),
    ('22659', 3, $$When mummy returned, She was so happy to see her children at home. She opened the kitchen so that she could serve them food. She found grains of rice allover the kitchen.$$),
    ('22659', 4, $$She called all her children to the kitchen and asked them about the food. Junior was the first to deny but his shirt was stained with soup.$$),
    ('22659', 5, $$Mummy served the remaining food and purnished junior by denying him lunch. She told him never to do it again$$),
    ('22675', 0, $$This is our process for helping people turn a story into a picture story book.$$),
    ('22675', 1, $$We offer storybook development and other workshops with our partners in multiple African countries.$$),
    ('22675', 2, $$We work with illustrators from concept to final colour.$$),
    ('22675', 3, $$We encourage cultural rather than literal translation of storybooks.$$),
    ('22675', 4, $$We help writers improve their work so that their stories can be approved.$$),
    ('22675', 5, $$We support people to read our storybooks offline or online or from print copies.$$),
    ('22675', 6, $$We can print and deliver large numbers of storybooks very cost effectively, with the help of our publishing network.$$),
    ('22675', 7, $$And we also create material for educators, for example, these Guides, which you can download, print and adapt.$$),
    ('22675', 8, $$There is no point in having storybooks if you don't use them with children.$$),
