    ('23165', 0, $$Knock, Knock! There is a loud rap on the front door.$$),
    ('23165', 1, $$Alake is very excited. "Who is there? ", she yells.$$),
    ('23165', 2, $$"Okay," her mum replies. "That would be Mrs. Hassan and her baby. Please open the door for them. "$$),
    ('23165', 3, $$Alake opens the door with a smile.$$),
    ('23165', 4, $$"Please have some water ma'am. Can I get you anything else? " asks Alake.$$),
    ('23165', 5, $$"Good day Mrs. Hassan," says Alake's mum as she comes into the sitting room. "Sorry I kept you waiting"$$),
    ('23165', 6, $$Alake's mum beams a smile at her.$$),
    ('23172', 0, $$Long time ago, Mojo the bird lived in a kingdom called Baro. Tortoise was his friend.$$),
    ('23172', 1, $$One day the lion king called for Mojo.$$),
    ('23172', 2, $$When Tortoise heard about this he felt jealous.$$),
    ('23172', 3, $$Mojo felt scared. The next day, he went to his relative Cock. Mojo told him what Tortoise had said.$$),
    ('23172', 4, $$"I will make you a delicious meal tonight," offered Tortoise when he met Mojo later.$$),
    ('23172', 5, $$As Mojo was relaxing, Tortoise threw a net over him! Then he went out to fetch firewood.$$),
    ('23172', 6, $$Cock was near Tortoise's house. He heard Mojo shouting. He called "Mojo? Mojo? "$$),
    ('23172', 7, $$Cock freed Mojo from the net.$$),
    ('23187', 0, $$Water has a lot of importance.$$),
    ('23187', 1, $$Water helps us to bath.$$),
    ('23187', 2, $$Water helps us to wash.$$),
    ('23187', 3, $$Children play with water.$$),
    ('23187', 4, $$Water helps us to cook and drink.$$),
    ('23187', 5, $$Some swim in it.$$),
    ('23187', 6, $$All animals love water.$$),
    ('23187', 7, $$But ponds are dangerous, we deserve to stay away from them.$$),
    ('23220', 0, $$Mark leaves home to go the zoo with his friend Mary.$$),
    ('23220', 1, $$On their way, Mary and Mark buy eats.$$),
    ('23220', 2, $$Mary develops a bad headache and she starts crying.$$),
    ('23220', 3, $$Mark looks for a near by clinic to get medicine.$$),
    ('23220', 4, $$He finds there is no doctor or nurse.$$),
    ('23220', 5, $$They had gone to talk to the people about living a healthy life.$$),
    ('23220', 6, $$Mark shouted for help and people called the doctor.$$),
    ('23220', 7, $$Mark gave Mary the doctor's medicine and took her back home.$$),
    ('23220', 8, $$So he did not get to see the animals, because his friend was sick.$$),
    ('2327', 0, $$Long, long ago there were seven suns in the sky. Their rays made the earth so hot that human beings could not bear it.$$),
    ('2327', 1, $$So, the seven brothers belonging to a tribe called the Munda decided to kill the suns. They shot arrows at them and were able to kill six of the suns.$$),
    ('2327', 2, $$The seventh sun hid behind a hill.$$),
    ('2327', 3, $$Now, with the suns gone, there was darkness everywhere. The deer could not see the tigers. The elephants bumped into trees. The rabbits walked over the lions and there was confusion all around.$$),
    ('2327', 4, $$To find a solution, the animals decided to have a meeting. A rabbit told them about one of the seven suns who was still alive and hiding behind a hill. But who would be the best one to call back the sun?$$),
    ('2327', 5, $$"I will call out to the sun," said the lion, for he was the king of the forest. "Sun, sun, please do not run away. Come back and shine on us," roared the lion. But the sun did not listen to him.$$),
    ('2327', 6, $$The elephant called out next. He raised his trunk and trumpeted, "Sun, sun, please come back. " But the sun did not listen to him.$$),
    ('2327', 7, $$The peacock danced and pleaded, "Sun, sun, please come back. " But the sun refused to come out. One after the other, all the animals called out to the sun. But he did not listen to any of them. Finally, a rooster offered to call the sun. Everybody laughed.$$),
    ('2327', 8, $$The lion said, "The rooster should be allowed to try. " Rooster stepped forward and gently crowed, "Kookoodokoo, koo. " To everyone's surprise, the sun peeped out a little from behind the hill.$$),
    ('2327', 9, $$The rooster crowed again, this time more boldly, "KookoodoKOO-KOO. " The sun came up a little higher. The rooster crowed a third time, even louder and bolder, "KOOKOODOKOO-KOO. " The sun rose high and bright. There was light everywhere. The animals were delighted and the humans felt relieved.$$),
    ('2327', 10, $$Ever since that day, when the rooster crows in the morning, the sun rises and shines in the sky.$$),
    ('23405', 0, $$Once, there lived a farmer who had two little sons. They were called Momoh and Sorie. The boys were twins.$$),
    ('23405', 1, $$Each time, the boys took rice with them.$$),
    ('23405', 2, $$Whenever Momoh and Sorie passed by the forest, Monkey threatened them.$$),
    ('23405', 3, $$Momoh and Sorie were afraid. They gave the rice to Monkey and he ate it hungrily. Monkey told the boys not to tell their father.$$),
    ('23405', 4, $$The two boys became thinner by the day. Their father noticed this and asked them why it was so.$$),
    ('23405', 5, $$"He says if we don't give him the rice, he will do something bad to us," Sorie said crying as well.$$),
    ('23405', 6, $$"Monkey says we should not tell you," the boys replied together.$$),
    ('23405', 7, $$Momoh and Sorie remembered that quite well and they kept quiet.$$),
    ('23405', 8, $$When Momoh and Sorie next went to the farm, they carried a bigger basket with them.$$),
    ('23405', 9, $$Monkey threatened the boys. He said, "You small boys, give me what you have in that basket. If you do not, I will do something bad to you. "$$),
    ('23405', 10, $$When Monkey opened the basket, a big dog jumped out and bit him!$$),
    ('23405', 11, $$Since that time, Monkey has not returned.$$),
    ('23430', 0, $$My name is Bongiwe. I live in a town called Mpophomeni in KwaZulu-Natal.$$),
    ('23430', 1, $$I have a small dog called Scruffy.$$),
    ('23430', 2, $$After school on Fridays, Scruffy and I go to Funda Nenja classes with lots of other children and their dogs.$$),
    ('23430', 3, $$Funda Nenja means learning with the dog in isiZulu.$$),
    ('23430', 4, $$Funda Nenja teaches us how to care for our dogs in a kind and gentle way.$$),
    ('23430', 5, $$Funda Nenja teaches the dogs how to jump over logs and run through tunnels.$$),
    ('23430', 6, $$Scruffy and the other dogs enjoy their happy times together.$$),
    ('23430', 7, $$I love Scruffy and Scruffy loves me.$$),
    ('23686', 0, $$In a lakeside village, there lived a young girl.$$),
    ('23686', 1, $$Atieno's father was a famous fisherman. He had his own little fishing canoe.$$),
    ('23686', 2, $$Atieno loved to watch the boys play football.$$),
    ('23686', 3, $$Girls said to Atieno, "Your legs are too long. " Atieno was sad.$$),
    ('23686', 4, $$One day, the sun did not rise. Cocks did not crow, birds did not sing and children did not go to school. Even her father did not go fishing.$$),
    ('23686', 5, $$Atieno went to the other children to tell them how sad she was. But they laughed at her, "Perhaps your friend the sun, is dead. Or perhaps it ran away from you. "$$),
    ('23686', 6, $$Atieno was so sad that she ran to the house. She kicked her brother's ball very hard.$$),
    ('23686', 7, $$Atieno thought, "I will play with this ball until my friend, the sun returns. "$$),
    ('23686', 8, $$When Atieno reached outside, she put the ball on the ground. She thought, "I can play just like them. "$$),
    ('23686', 9, $$As everybody stared up, the ball disappeared into the thick clouds.$$),
    ('23686', 10, $$Suddenly, the thick clouds cleared and the sun appeared. Life returned to the lakeside village once again.$$),
    ('23686', 11, $$Atieno could not believe that she had brought the sun back to the village. But most of all, the sun was back in her life.$$),
    ('23798', 0, $$Once there lived a boy called Otto who lived with his mother Acan.$$),
    ('23798', 1, $$They lived in a hut and were very happy together$$),
    ('23798', 2, $$Hunger fell in the land and the mother was worried about what her son was to eat so she decided to leave for a few days in search for food. she promised to return.$$),
    ('23798', 3, $$Acan moved for many days in search for food for her son Otto till she got where to buy food from.$$),
    ('23798', 4, $$Otto watched the sun rise and set as he waited for his mothers return.$$),
    ('23798', 5, $$One day a woman approached Otto and gave him what to eat. She told him that the mother had said he should go live with her as she is to take more days in the search for food.$$),
    ('23798', 6, $$Otto forgot his mother's warning about strangers as to the fact that the stranger had spoken the purpose to why the mother was away. He moved with the stranger till they reached the forest.$$),
    ('23798', 7, $$At the forest, Otto thought about his mothers stories of children being eaten in the forest and decided to ask the stranger for an excuse to go urinate. With that he left and run away as fast as he could. The witch realized but as she begun to chase after Otto, he had reached the village where she was banished so she could not go in.$$),
    ('23798', 8, $$Acan returned to the news of her son's survival of the witch's cunning trick. that brought joy in the village as it brought bravery to the other children.$$),
    ('23860', 0, $$There lived a set of twin brothers called Elephant and Hippo.$$),
    ('23860', 1, $$Hippo became an angry boy because of the leprosy. Blindness gave Elephant patience. Elephant needed help but Hippo treated him badly.$$),
    ('23860', 2, $$Because Hippo lost his fingers and toes he also needed help. Elephant was kind to him.$$),
    ('23860', 3, $$One morning, their mother served hot yams. Because they were hungry, the boys did not wait for the yams to cool.$$),
    ('23860', 4, $$The yam was so hot that it burned their palms. Hippo moved his hands up and down, to cool his palms. The yam fell on the sand.$$),
    ('23860', 5, $$"Help me pick up my yam, please," Hippo asked Elephant.$$),
    ('23860', 6, $$In the evening, their mother gave them pounded yam, and four pieces of meat. Elephant quickly touched the meat and counted the pieces with his fingers.$$),
    ('23860', 7, $$Hippo tried to steal a piece of meat but he dropped it. When it was time to share the meat, Elephant asked, "Were there not four pieces of meat? "$$),
    ('23860', 8, $$One day, they were hungry but their mother was away. "Let us go and play to forget our hunger," suggested Hippo.$$),
    ('23860', 9, $$On the way, Hippo went into the forest. He picked up a heavy stone and put it under his arm. He grunted as he picked up the stone. He was still grunting as they walked to the river.$$),
    ('23860', 10, $$When they reached the river, Hippo said, "I will jump in first and help you to swim. "$$),
    ('23860', 11, $$Elephant asked Stick to find out more. He started swinging Stick round and round. Stick hit Hippo very hard. "Ayei! " Hippo cried and jumped into the river.$$),
    ('23861', 0, $$One day, mother was cooking. She called, "Abena, Ajua, Akwasi, Aku, food is ready! Bring your plates! "$$),
    ('23861', 1, $$Aku looked at her fufu. She looked at her soup. Then she looked at her meat. She put her plate down angrily and cried. Mother asked, "Aku, what is wrong with you? "$$),
    ('23861', 2, $$Aku cried harder. She threw herself on the ground. Mother did not want Aku to cry. She called loudly, "Abena, Ajua, Akwasi, bring your food quickly! "$$),
    ('23861', 3, $$Aku smiled happily. She looked hard at Akwasi's meat and thought, "Akwasi's meat is big. "$$),
    ('23861', 4, $$"Abena, give your meat to Aku and take hers," mother said. Abena was unhappy. But she did not want to disobey mother. So she gave her meat to Aku.$$),
    ('23861', 5, $$Pii! Pii! It was the ice cream man outside. He rode a big bicycle with a small horn.$$),
    ('23861', 6, $$"Four ice creams, please," mother said to the ice cream man. She paid and gave each child an ice cream.$$),
    ('23861', 7, $$She looked harder at Ajua's ice cream. It was the smallest of all. She was happy. "My ice cream is the biggest," she thought.$$),
    ('23861', 8, $$"What is the matter, Aku? " mother asked.$$),
    ('23861', 9, $$Mother said to Abena, "Please give your ice cream to Aku and take hers. "$$),
    ('23861', 10, $$"Mmmm, this ice cream looks good," said Aku. She opened her mouth wide and took a big bite.$$),
    ('23861', 11, $$Mother took Aku's ice cream and bit into it. She frowned. "There is something wrong with this. It is spoilt," she said.$$),
    ('23862', 0, $$A bajaj is a three-wheeled vehicle.$$),
    ('23862', 1, $$In our community, driving is a male-dominated activity.$$),
    ('23862', 2, $$One day, Abebech asked her parents to give her money to take driving lessons.$$),
    ('23862', 3, $$However, Abebech said, "I have the ability to do anything other people do. " She convinced them.$$),
    ('23862', 4, $$Abebech successfully completed the training.$$),
    ('23862', 5, $$Afterwards they agreed to buy a bajaj for her.$$),
    ('23862', 6, $$One day Abebech had a great idea.$$),
    ('23862', 7, $$The notice read, "I transport for free, pregnant women, mothers who have delivered, and children. "$$),
    ('23862', 8, $$Abebech earned money by transporting people.$$),
    ('23862', 9, $$Abebech was very happy with her work. The elderly blessed her. Everybody talked about her work all the time.$$),
    ('23936', 0, $$Once upon a time there were three little boys and one girl. They all lived in one village called Nyabunkaka.$$),
    ('23936', 1, $$They were studying at Glory Children's Academy primary school Jalingo.$$),
    ('23936', 2, $$On their way to school, they used to hear birds singing.$$),
    ('23936', 3, $$One day, three boys and one girl decided to dodge school and went for hunting in River Mayo Gwoi.$$),
    ('23936', 4, $$The bird started singing like this, You call me bad; I am not bad x4.$$),
    ('23936', 5, $$Am good but if you think am bad I will be bad at the end. Then the children caught that bird and went with it at home.$$),
    ('23936', 6, $$When they reached home they slaughtered and ate it.$$),
    ('23936', 7, $$The following day they all fell sick. Because its meat was poisonous.$$),
    ('23938', 0, $$Long ago, there was a famine in the land of Taraba. People were forced to dig in ant hills to find grains.$$),
    ('23938', 1, $$There was a man called Vyandeh who couldn't provide food for his family. One day, he said to his wife, "Let me go and look for food. " He left.$$),
    ('23938', 2, $$While he was away, the wife couldn't wait for his return. So she cooked some pumpkin that she got from her mother for the children, Vyangel, Avaungwa and Jessica.$$),
    ('23938', 3, $$Vyandeh come back late, with nothing for the family. The wife, Aershimana welcomed him and asked, "Anything for the children to eat? " He replied, "No. "$$),
    ('23938', 4, $$He asked, "Where did you get the pumpkin? "$$),
    ('23938', 5, $$At midnight, Vyandeh woke up.$$),
    ('23938', 6, $$He put up the ladder and climbed to the old woman's roof.$$),
    ('23938', 7, $$Vyandeh began to walk across the roof towards the pumpkins.$$),
    ('23938', 8, $$He stood up quickly.$$),
    ('23938', 9, $$The old woman got a huge fright. She was terrified.$$),
    ('23938', 10, $$She rushed to the door to escape. But Vyandeh suddenly ran out before her.$$),
    ('23938', 11, $$Both crying for help, Vyandeh ran eastward and the old woman ran westward.$$),
    ('23944', 0, $$In a village on the slopes of Mambilla Plateau in North-East Nigeria, a little girl worked in the fields with her mother. Her name was Wanderimam.$$),
    ('23944', 1, $$Wanderimam loved being outside. In her family's food garden she broke up the soil with her machete. She pressed tiny seeds into the warm earth.$$),
    ('23944', 2, $$Her favourite time of day was just after sunset. When it got too dark to see the plants, Wanderimam knew it was time to go home. She would follow the narrow paths through the fields, crossing rivers as she went.$$),
    ('23944', 3, $$Wanderiam was a clever child and couldn't wait to go to school. But her mother and father wanted her to stay and help them at home. When she was seven years old, her big brother persuaded her parents to let her go to school.$$),
    ('23944', 4, $$She liked to learn! Wanderimam learnt more and more with every book she read. She did so well at school that she was invited to study at Ahmadu Bello University, Zaria. Wanderimam was excited! She wanted to know more about the world.$$),
    ('23944', 5, $$At the Ahmadu Bello University, Zaria, Wanderimam learnt many new things. She studied plants and how they grow. And she remembered how she grew: playing games with her brothers in the shade of the trees in the beautiful Kurmi forests.$$),
    ('23944', 6, $$The more she learnt, the more she realised that she loved the people of Taraba. She wanted them to be happy and free. The more she learnt, the more she remembered her Mambilla home.$$),
    ('23944', 7, $$When she had finished her studies, she returned to Taraba, her State. But her whole country had changed. Huge farms stretched across the land. Women had no wood to make cooking fires. The people were poor and the children were hungry.$$),
    ('23944', 8, $$Wanderimam knew what to do. She taught the women how to plant trees from seeds. The women sold the trees and used the money to look after their families. The women were very happy. Wanderimam had helped them to feel powerful and strong.$$),
    ('23944', 9, $$As time passed, the new trees grew into forests, and the rivers started flowing again. Wanderimam's message spread across Nigeria. Today, millions of trees have grown from Wanderimam's seeds.$$),
    ('23944', 10, $$Wanderimam had worked hard. People all over Nigeria took notice, and gave her a famous prize. It is called the Nigerian Conservation Prize, and she was the first Kuteb woman ever to receive it.$$),
    ('23944', 11, $$Wanderimam got married in 2018. We can think of her every time we see a beautiful tree.$$)
  ) AS v(source_id, page_index, text)
  JOIN books b
    ON b.source_platform = 'african_storybook' AND b.source_id = v.source_id
ON CONFLICT (book_id, page_index) DO NOTHING;

-- ───────── [적재검증] 트랜잭션 안 ─────────
-- (c) 적재 후 행 수 — 기대 508
SELECT count(*) AS rows_after FROM book_text bt
  JOIN books b ON b.id = bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('22191', '22194', '22256', '22261', '22315', '22325', '22357', '22410', '22481', '22510', '22542', '22550', '22565', '22599', '22602', '22642', '22659', '22675', '22679', '22702', '22706', '22741', '22772', '2278', '22870', '22971', '22996', '23002', '23015', '23064', '23116', '23135', '23150', '23165', '23172', '23187', '23220', '2327', '23405', '23430', '23686', '23798', '23860', '23861', '23862', '23936', '23938', '23944');
-- (d) 조인 실패로 누락된 source_id — 기대 0행
SELECT v.source_id FROM (VALUES ('22191'), ('22194'), ('22256'), ('22261'), ('22315'), ('22325'), ('22357'), ('22410'), ('22481'), ('22510'), ('22542'), ('22550'), ('22565'), ('22599'), ('22602'), ('22642'), ('22659'), ('22675'), ('22679'), ('22702'), ('22706'), ('22741'), ('22772'), ('2278'), ('22870'), ('22971'), ('22996'), ('23002'), ('23015'), ('23064'), ('23116'), ('23135'), ('23150'), ('23165'), ('23172'), ('23187'), ('23220'), ('2327'), ('23405'), ('23430'), ('23686'), ('23798'), ('23860'), ('23861'), ('23862'), ('23936'), ('23938'), ('23944')) AS v(source_id)
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM books b
     WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id=v.source_id);
-- (e) source 라벨 분포 — 기대 manifest_txt_v1 1종 / 508행
SELECT bt.source, count(*) FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('22191', '22194', '22256', '22261', '22315', '22325', '22357', '22410', '22481', '22510', '22542', '22550', '22565', '22599', '22602', '22642', '22659', '22675', '22679', '22702', '22706', '22741', '22772', '2278', '22870', '22971', '22996', '23002', '23015', '23064', '23116', '23135', '23150', '23165', '23172', '23187', '23220', '2327', '23405', '23430', '23686', '23798', '23860', '23861', '23862', '23936', '23938', '23944')
  GROUP BY bt.source;
-- (f) page_index 축 검증 — 권마다 0부터 연속이어야 한다. 기대 0행(위반 없음)
SELECT b.source_id, min(bt.page_index) AS mn, max(bt.page_index) AS mx, count(*) AS n
  FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('22191', '22194', '22256', '22261', '22315', '22325', '22357', '22410', '22481', '22510', '22542', '22550', '22565', '22599', '22602', '22642', '22659', '22675', '22679', '22702', '22706', '22741', '22772', '2278', '22870', '22971', '22996', '23002', '23015', '23064', '23116', '23135', '23150', '23165', '23172', '23187', '23220', '2327', '23405', '23430', '23686', '23798', '23860', '23861', '23862', '23936', '23938', '23944')
  GROUP BY b.source_id HAVING min(bt.page_index) <> 0 OR max(bt.page_index) <> count(*)-1;
-- (g) 빈 면 행 수 — 기대 0 (D7: 빈 면도 text='' 로 적재)
SELECT count(*) AS empty_rows FROM book_text bt JOIN books b ON b.id=bt.book_id
  WHERE b.source_platform='african_storybook' AND b.source_id IN ('22191', '22194', '22256', '22261', '22315', '22325', '22357', '22410', '22481', '22510', '22542', '22550', '22565', '22599', '22602', '22642', '22659', '22675', '22679', '22702', '22706', '22741', '22772', '2278', '22870', '22971', '22996', '23002', '23015', '23064', '23116', '23135', '23150', '23165', '23172', '23187', '23220', '2327', '23405', '23430', '23686', '23798', '23860', '23861', '23862', '23936', '23938', '23944') AND bt.text = '';

-- ───────── [종료] ─────────
-- (c)~(g)가 전부 기대값과 일치하면 아래 ROLLBACK; 을 COMMIT; 으로 바꿔 타이핑한다.
